problems.problem:
We place red and blue balls on a circle. At the beginning there are only two red balls on the circle. The following moves are allowed:

i) add one red ball and change the colour of each of the two balls adjacent to it (red to blue and vice versa);

ii) remove one red ball and change the colour of each of the two balls adjacent to it.

Can we achieve by a sequence of such moves that there are on the circle

a) $2013$ red and $2013$ blue balls;

b) only two blue balls?

Let $N$ be a positive integer and let $S=\{1,2,\dots,N\}$. Let $a_{i,j}$ be pairwise distinct real numbers such that for all $i,j\in S$: if $i<j$, then $$a_{i,k}<a_{j,k}\quad\text{and}\quad a_{k,i}<a_{k,j}\qquad\text{for all } k\in S.$$ Let $n$ be a positive integer such that $2(n-1)^2<N$. Prove that there exist $n$-element subsets $I,J\subset S$ such that one of the following two statements holds:

- for all $i,k\in I$: if $i<k$, then $a_{i,j}<a_{k,l}$ for all $j,l\in J$;

- for all $j,l\in J$: if $j<l$, then $a_{i,j}<a_{k,l}$ for all $i,k\in I$.
